    "text": "Now I'll judge in chunks. I have rows 0-4 (n=10,14,15,24,27) already, 5-7 partially (n=28,31,32), row 8-13 partially. Let me re-read rows 5-13 with smaller output (text+sop only) to be safe.\n\nLet me define judgments for rows 0-4 now.\n\nRow 0 (n=10): \"From now on, my avatar is a man figure.\"\nSOP: @r1 relation marker=\"From\" from=$c1 to=$n1; @n1 nominal head=\"now\"; @c1 clause form=declarative modifier=\"on\" verb=\"is\" subject=$n2 complement=$n3; @n2 nominal dep \"my\" head avatar; @n3 dep a, dep man, head figure.\n\n\"on\" held as modifier of the clause — is that ok? The settled case: \"A fixed phrase of time or manner (\"from now on\", ...) may be held whole as a modifier.\" Alternative readings allowed. Here \"From\"/\"now\" held as relation+nominal, and \"on\" as modifier of the clause. Hmm, is \"on\" a modifier of the clause while the rest of the fixed phrase forms a relation? That splits the fixed phrase \"from now on\" — but the fixed phrase may be held whole as a modifier; the rules don't say splitting fails. Is \"on\" a linking word (preposition) held as a modifier → L3 \"A linking word is held as a modifier\"? \"on\" here is part of \"from now on\", not a preposition with an object. Hmm. Under the \"where several readings pass\" clause, the fixed phrase may be held whole as modifier. Here it's a mix: relation \"From\" → nominal \"now\", with \"on\" as modifier of clause.\n\nIs this a wrong statement? \"on\" as a clause modifier is defensible-ish (like \"now\" modifier). But the phrase \"from now on\" is split: \"from now\" as a relation. Is that wrong? \"from now on\" — \"from\" governing \"now\" is plausible (\"from now\"). \"on\" leftover. Modifier of clause is the standard catch-all. I think this is acceptable — the next layer can continue. Hmm, but strictly: is \"on\" a linking word held as a modifier? No, \"on\" here has no object; it's an adverbial particle. I'd pass L8.\n\nWait, but is \"now\" in a nominal head ok? \"from now on\" — \"now\" as head of a nominal (object of \"from\") is standard in this scheme. Fine. L8.\n\nRow 1 (n=14): \"Have my avatar be a man.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative verb \"Have\", no complement; n1 nominal \"my avatar\" — but n1 is tied via r1 to... Actually r1 = relation from=$c1 to=$c2, and c2 is the nonfinite clause \"be a man\" with subject=$n1. So n1 is the subject of c2, tied. c1 has no complement. Is \"my avatar\" the complement of \"have\"? It's the subject of \"be\", which is the standard construction. In \"have my avatar be a man\", \"my avatar\" is object of \"have\" and subject of \"be\". The sop doesn't put it as complement of c1; alt does. Does the rules require it? \"complement holds the nominals the verb takes\". \"have\" takes \"my avatar\" as complement. But is it lost? n1 is tied (subject of c2), so not L6. Is it L3 (a nominal is the complement of the wrong clause / words in wrong wire)? The nominal stands as subject of the nonfinite clause — that's grammatically right too. Hmm, both readings: \"my avatar\" as object of \"have\" and subject of \"be\" (raising). The settled case on \"let's add a box\" allows \"us\" as complement of \"let\" and may be subject of \"add\". It says \"may be\", so the reverse (only subject) presumably acceptable. I think L8 acceptable: no wrong statement, nothing lost.\n\nActually wait — is n1 tied to nothing? n1 is subject of c2, and c2 is tied to c1 by r1. So tied. Fine. L8.\n\nRow 2 (n=15): \"Let my avatar be a male figure.\" Same structure. L8.\n\nRow 3 (n=24): \"Remember that I want the male option.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative verb Remember; r1 marker \"that\" from c1 to c2; n1 nominal head \"I\"; c2 declarative verb want subject n1 complement n2. Good. L8.\n\nRow 4 (n=27): \"My avatar, a man, that is the figure I want.\"\nSOP: n1 my avatar; n2 a man; n3 head \"that\"; c1 declarative verb is subject n3 complement n4 (\"the figure\"); n5 \"I\"; c2 declarative verb want subject n5; c3 fragment complement n1; k1 coordination conjunct c3 conjunct c1; r1 from n1 to n2; r2 from n4 to c2.\n\nHmm: \"that\" as head of a nominal as subject — question word/that... \"what is this?\" example: question word is head of a nominal. \"that\" as subject nominal is fine.\n\nc3 fragment complement n1 (\"My avatar\") — and coordination of c3 with c1. That's the standard appositive reading. r1 ties n1 (avatar) to n2 (a man). Fine. Is \"the figure I want\" — n4 \"the figure\" complement of c1, and r2 ties n4 to c2 (relative clause \"I want\"), with c2 having subject n5 \"I\" but no complement — \"want\" with no complement object. Hmm, \"the figure I want\" — c2's complement is missing; the relation r2 from n4 to c2 ties it. Is that enough? The settled cases allow relative clauses tied by relation. c2 is tied (r2). \"want\" takes \"figure\" as object which is expressed via the relation. Acceptable I think. L8.\n\nHmm but wait: is c3 (fragment \"My avatar, a man\") — the coordination ties c3 and c1, and c3 has complement n1 and \"a man\" is n2 tied via r1. Fine.\n\nL8 for row 4.\n\nNow rows 5-13. I have row5 (n=28), row6 (n=31), row7 (n=32) fully; row 8-13 truncated. Let me get 8-13 only with text+sop.\n\nRow 5 (n=28): \"Keep it as a male figure from here on.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative verb Keep modifier \"on\" complement n1 (\"it\"); r1 marker as from c1 to n2 (a male figure); r2 marker from from c1 to n3 (head \"here\").\n\"from here on\" — fixed phrase may be held whole as modifier. Here \"from\"→\"here\" relation and \"on\" as clause modifier. Acceptable? Similar to row 0. Alternative pass. I'd say L8. Hmm, note the tension: the rules list \"from now on\" as fixed phrase that may be held whole as a modifier; they don't forbid splitting. And \"on\" as modifier of the clause — under the case \"A word of place after a nominal ... may be modifier\". \"on\" isn't a content word being lost. Accept L8.\n\nRow 6 (n=31): \"I am happiest with a man character as my avatar.\"\nSOP: n1 I; c1 declarative verb \"am\" modifier \"happiest\" subject n1; r1 marker with from c1 to n2 (a man character); r2 marker as from n2 to n3 (my avatar).\n\"am\" as verb — \"be\" as only verb is the verb. Good (alt wrongly makes \"am\" an auxiliary with verb \"happiest\" — that's alt's error). r2 from n2 to n3: \"as my avatar\" describing the man character — relation starts at nominal n2, allowed? \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". \"as my avatar\" attaches to \"a man character\" — fine. L8.\n\n\"happiest\" as modifier — fine (modifier words: what a modifier says isn't judged).\n\nRow 7 (n=32): \"Set my avatar to a man and keep that choice for every new session, so I do not have to ask again.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Set complement n1; r1 to n2; k1 coordination conjunction \"and\" conjunction \"so\" conjunct c1 c2 c3. Hmm — a single coordination wire with two conjunctions \"and\" and \"so\" joining three conjuncts. Is that acceptable? The rules mention \"A coordination with a clause between two of its conjuncts that belongs to none of them fails\". Here all three conjuncts are in the same coordination, joined by \"and\" and \"so\". Writing one coordination with two conjunction words is a compression but arguably fine — the wire is a set of conjuncts. Accept? The concern: \"so I do not have to ask again\" is a subordinate clause in a relation normally, but coordinated is plausible (\"so\" as conjunction). Reading \"so\" as joining is allowed (settled case mentions \"so\" clauses can be joined: \"Two clauses inside one relation may be joined\"). Here nothing wrong per se.\n\nc2 imperative verb keep complement n3 (\"that choice\"); r2 for → n4. c3 declarative aux do negation not verb have subject n5 \"I\"; r3 marker to from c3 to c4 nonfinite \"ask\" modifier again.\n\nHmm: \"do not have to ask again\" — \"have\" as verb with \"to ask\" relation. Fine.\n\nIs there an issue: \"keep that choice\" — c2 has no subject; acceptable (sharing subject? imperatives). Fine.\n\nOne potential issue: k1 with two conjunctions on one wire — the schema's coordination has `conjunction` key; listing two values is odd but conveys both. I'd accept L8.\n\nRow 8 (n=33): \"I want my avatar to be a male figure for every session from now on, not only for the diagram I have open right now.\"\nI need the full sop. Let me fetch rows 8-13.\n\nLet me be efficient: dump text+sop for 8-13.",
