    "text": "Row 11 (n=37): \"I would like my avatar to be a man character, so please set it and keep it for later instead of resetting it.\"\nSOP: k1 coordination conjunction \"so\" conjunction \"and\" conjunct c1, c3, c4. c1 declarative; c3 imperative set; c4 imperative keep; r3 instead of → c5 nonfinite resetting.\nCoordination joining declarative c1 with imperatives — is that allowed? The rules: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause [a clause that ends a relation with a marker] has a wrong conjunct: L3.\"\n\nHere, the coordination joins c1 (declarative, main clause \"I would like...\"), c3 and c4 (imperatives). Is a coordination of a declarative with imperatives wrong? The prohibition was for clauses inside a relation. \"so\" here: \"so please set it\" — \"so\" as conjunction joining the clause. This is a \"so\" that connects two sentences/clauses; the schema options: relation marked \"so\" or coordination. Both accepted per the settled case about \"and after that\". Here coordination with declarative + imperative conjuncts: is a command able to be a conjunct of a coordination with a declarative? Nothing forbids it explicitly. The rule about imperative conjunct only covers clauses ending a relation with a marker. I'd accept. But wait: is c5 \"resetting it\" inside relation r3 from c4 → c5; c5 is a nonfinite clause, fine (not imperative/interrogative).\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: r3 from c4 to c5 \"instead of resetting it\". Fine.\n\nL8 for row 11? Let me check \"keep it for later\" — n6 \"later\" head of nominal, relation \"for\". Fine. \"please\" in discourse of c3 — fine.\n\nOne concern: \"set it\" and \"keep it\" complements \"it\" — fine.\n\nL8.\n\nRow 12 (n=38): \"Make my avatar the male figure; that is the figure I want every time I open the app or start a new drawing.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Make complement n1 n2; n3 \"that\"; c2 declarative is subject n3 complement n4; n5 I; c3 declarative want subject n5 complement n6 (\"every time\")?? Hmm \"want every time I open...\" — c3 verb want subject n5 complement n6 \"every time\"; c4 declarative open subject n7 complement n8; k1 coordination \"or\" c4 c5 (start); c5 declarative start complement n9; k2 coordination c1 c2; r1 from n4 to c3; r2 from n6 to k1.\n\nIssues: \"want\" is given complement n6 \"every time\" and \"every time I open the app or start a new drawing\" — the figure wanted. Hmm, \"the figure I want every time I open the app\" — \"want\" takes \"the figure\" as object which is r1 from n4 to c3. c3's complement is n6 \"every time\" — that's a time adjunct treated as complement. Not judged (what a complement is to the verb is not judged). Fine.\n\nk1 coordination \"or\" joining c4 and c5: c5 has no subject — sharing subject \"I\"? c5 declarative verb start, no subject. Per settled case, a verb after \"and\"/\"then\" following a clause with subject and auxiliary may share them. \"open the app or start a new drawing\" — no auxiliary, but sharing subject for \"or\" coordination is normal. Subject omission: is it L4 (subject the words do not show)? The words do show the shared subject through coordination. Acceptable? The settled case covers \"and\"/\"then\" with auxiliary. For \"or\" with present tense verbs, subject sharing is standard grammar. I'd accept.\n\nk2 joins c1 and c2 without conjunction (semicolon). Fine.\n\nWait: \"that is the figure I want\" — \"that\" as subject nominal head. OK.\n\nHmm, one thing: c5 has no complement? \"start a new drawing\" - complement n9. ok.\n\nL8 for row 12.\n\nRow 13 (n=40): \"I would like my avatar to stay a male figure no matter which diagram or drawing I open, so please remember it for the next session too.\"\nSOP: c2 nonfinite stay modifier \"no\" modifier \"matter\" subject n2 complement n3; n4 \"which diagram\"; k1 \"or\" n4 n5; c3 declarative open subject n6 (\"I\") complement k1; k2 \"so\" c1 c4; r3 from c2 to c3.\n\n\"no matter which diagram\" — held as modifiers \"no\" \"matter\" and then \"which diagram or drawing I open\" as a clause tied to c2 by r3. Plausible. \"too\" as modifier of c4. Fine.\n\nIs anything wrong? c3 \"open\" has subject n6 and complement k1 (a coordination of nominals \"which diagram or drawing\"). Fine.\n\nL8.\n\nRow 14 (n=41): \"Could you set my avatar to the man and keep that figure every time I start a new drawing, instead of asking me again?\"\nSOP: c1 interrogative aux Could verb set subject n1 complement n2; r1 to n3; k1 \"and\" conjunct c1, c2, c5; c2 interrogative verb keep (no subject — shared with c1's \"you\"); c5 fragment ellipsis=c2 — what is c5? \"instead of asking me again\" — hmm, c5 is a fragment with ellipsis to c2. And r2 \"instead of\" from c5 to c4 (nonfinite asking). c5 seems to represent an elided clause \"instead of asking me again\" that shares the verb \"keep\"? Hmm.\n\nActually the structure: \"keep that figure every time I start a new drawing, instead of asking me again\" — \"instead of asking me again\" is an adjunct of \"keep\". The sop writes c5 as a fragment with ellipsis=c2 (sharing \"keep\"), coordinated as a third conjunct, and then r2 from c5 to c4 (the nonfinite). That's inventing an elliptical clause \"keep ... instead of asking me again\"? That's odd: c5 is a fragment sharing the verb of c2 while having no words of its own except... wait every word must be in exactly one value. c5 has no words at all! A clause with no words. Is that a wrong statement (L4: a coordination between wires the message doesn't relate / ellipsis on a fragment that shares no verb)?\n\nHmm, c5 has form=fragment, ellipsis=c2, and no words. The judgement: \"Whether a fragment shares the verb of another clause (ellipsis)\" is #3 in what must be right. An empty fragment representing an elided repetition... Suspicious. The words \"instead of asking me again\" form c4 (nonfinite) with relation \"instead of\" from c5 to c4. But the start of the relation should be the clause c2 (keep) or a nominal of it — \"the start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause\". A start at an empty fragment clause c5 which itself is an ellipsis of c2... arguably it stands for the clause. But is an empty clause a wrong statement? It states that there's an elided clause which shares \"keep\". Hmm.\n\nIs it L3 \"A relation has ... a start the words rule out\"? Starting at an empty fragment that refers to the same clause... I'd say borderline. Hmm.\n\nAlso, k1 joins c1, c2, c5 — c5 being an empty clause joined as third conjunct. That says \"set my avatar to the man, and keep that figure, and (elided) instead of asking me again\" — this is a coordination with conjunects where the third has no words.\n\nAlternatively judge L8 (acceptable reading; the next layer can understand that \"instead of asking me again\" modifies an elided version of \"keep\"). It's not \"undoing a wrong statement\" — the empty fragment with ellipsis=c2 does carry the meaning that the adjunct belongs to \"keep\". Actually r2's from=c5=bogus, but c5 ≡ c2 semantically.\n\nHmm. I lean L8: the reading is recoverable, nothing lost, no wrong statement about words. But wait — is there any wrong statement? \"keep that figure every time I start a new drawing\" — n5 \"every time\" complement; r3 from n5 to c3. Fine. c2 has no subject: interrogative \"Could you set ... and keep\" shares \"you\". Per rules, sharing subject is allowed when clause has no subject and follows a question? The settled case is about declarative/imperative after \"and\". A second interrogative conjunct without subject is normal grammar (\"Could you set it and keep it?\"). Fine.\n\nI'd give L8. Hmm, but the empty clause bugs me. Let me reconsider: \"Words of one written clause are held in another\" (L1). Not applicable. \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" (L3) — is c5 a wrong conjunct? It's a clause with no words at all; the message has nothing coordinated. That's a bit like \"a relation or a coordination between wires that the message does not relate\" (L4). Hmm.\n\nHonestly, the intent of c5: the formalizer wanted to attach \"instead of asking me again\" as a relation, and needed a start; it couldn't start at c2 because... it could have. Creating an empty elliptical clause to host the relation is a formal device. I think a fair judge accepts — but it's arguably an invented clause (L4: \"The circuit states what the message does not say\"). The ellipsis case in L4 is \"ellipsis on a fragment that shares no verb ('move the box, and thanks')\". Here the empty fragment does share the verb of c2 — the claim is precisely that. So it's not that L4 case.\n\nI'll accept L8. Given the instruction \"Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable\".\n\nRow 15 (n=45): \"the male figure it is.\"\nSOP: n1 \"the male figure\"; n2 \"it\"; c1 declarative verb is subject n2 complement n1. Fine, L8. (Reading: \"the male figure it is\" — \"it is the male figure\". Order not judged.)\n\nRow 16 (n=46): \"a man from now on.\"\nSOP: n1 \"a man\"; r1 marker \"from\" from n1 to n2 (\"now\"); c1 fragment modifier \"on\" complement n1.\n\"a man from now on\" — fixed phrase \"from now on\" split: relation from n1 (the nominal!) to \"now\", with \"on\" as clause modifier. Hmm: relation marked \"from\" starting at n1 \"a man\" — \"The start of a relation with a marker: the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". Does grammar allow \"from\" to start at \"a man\"? \"a man from now on\" — \"from now on\" modifies the whole; attaching \"from now on\" to \"a man\" modifies the nominal (\"a man from now on\") which is arguably acceptable — it describes the man from now on. Hmm, but \"from now on\" is a time phrase, and the settled case says \"A word of time or order cannot describe a noun from where it stands\" — that was about a word of time as a dependent of a nominal. Here \"from now on\" as a relation from the nominal... L3 says a relation with \"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\". Starting at n1 (the only nominal) is grammatically not ruled out for \"from\" — \"the man from now on\"? Eh, weird but \"from now\" as a temporal prepositional phrase attaching to a nominal is odd.\n\nAlso, is \"on\" being a clause modifier wrong? It's a particle.\n\nHmm. Two sub-questions. This is one of those \"several readings pass\" gray zones. I think the safer judgement: the phrase \"from now on\" is a fixed time phrase; splitting it into a relation from the nominal plus \"on\" as a modifier of the fragment clause loses nothing explicit (all words present, all tied). There's no clause with a verb, so it's a fragment; complement n1; r1 from n1 (start at nominal — allowed by the \"start of a relation\" freedom) — pass. L8.\n\nWait, but is r1 tied? yes from n1 to n2. Nothing untied. L8.\n\nOK. Let me continue reading. I need to be efficient. Let me do 6 rows per dump: 17-22.\n\nBut I also need to record verdicts. Let me start appending to /tmp/verdicts in batches after every few dumps. Actually I'll accumulate and write parts of the final file directly into the target path using bash heredoc appends (>> after first part with >). That's writing the file in a few large parts — allowed. But careful: if I append and later need to fix, I'd have to rewrite. Let me collect verdicts in /tmp/v1.jsonl etc., then concatenate at the end into the target. Safer.\n\nActually simplest: I'll keep the judgments in scratchpad notes after each group of ~20 rows (compact JSON lines). Then final assembly from scratchpad reads. Hmm, reading back costs tokens.\n\nBest: append JSON lines to /tmp/part1.jsonl as I go with bash (cheap, one line per row, low tokens since I must type the content anyway). Then final: cat parts > target. Total typing = content once. Good.\n\nLet me record rows 0-16 now (17 rows: indices 0..16).\n\nGrades so far:\n0/n=10