    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Row 17 (n=50): \"Change that to a man character, please.\" — fine, L8.\n\nRow 18 (n=52): \"Use a man for the avatar from now on.\" — sop like row 5, \"on\" modifier on clause, \"from\"→\"now\". L8.\n\nRow 19 (n=53): \"I changed my mind: a male figure.\"\nSOP: c1 declarative changed; c2 fragment complement n3; k1 coordination (no conjunction) conjunct c1 c2. Fine — the colon creates a coordination or a relation. L8.\n\nRow 20 (n=54): \"Actually, set the avatar to the man and keep that instead of the one we just discussed.\"\nSOP: c2 imperative keep complement=$k2 where k2 is a coordination with conjunction \"instead of\" joining n3 \"that\" and n4 \"the one\". Hmm. \"keep that instead of the one we just discussed\" — \"instead of\" is a prepositional marker; treating it as a conjunction of a coordination between two nominals. Is that acceptable? L3: \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" or \"A linking word is held as a modifier or a dependent\" — not exactly. \"instead of\" as a conjunction joining two nominals: \"keep that instead of the one\" — the coordination of \"that\" and \"the one\" as the thing kept; that asserts both are kept, whereas \"instead of\" excludes. That's arguably a wrong statement (the words say keep that, not the other). Hmm. But the conjunction word \"instead of\" is on the coordination wire, so the exclusion is recorded. Does the schema allow \"instead of\" as conjunction? The coordination wire has `conjunction`. Grammar-wise \"instead of\" is a preposition, not a conjunction. L3 lists \"A relation has ... a `marker` that is no linking word\" for relations; for coordination: \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\".\n\nAlternatively, the acceptable reading: relation marked \"instead of\" from c2 to n4. The sop chose coordination. Hmm. Is this a \"wrong statement\"? The nominal \"the one we just discussed\" — the relation r2 ties n4 to c3, so it's not lost.\n\nLet me weigh: the judge's question: can the next layer continue, or must it undo a wrong statement? A coordination of \"that\" and \"the one\" with conjunction \"instead of\" — the conjunction word makes clear it's exclusion, not addition. I think this is a plausible encoding; the next layer isn't misled. But strictly, \"instead of\" is a preposition and a coordination of two nominals under it says they are put side by side...\n\nHmm, note in row 11 (n=37) the sop used relation marker \"instead of\" from c4 to c5 — proper. In row 20 it's a coordination. The rules don't explicitly cover \"preposition used as conjunction\". The closest: L3 \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct\" — are n3 and n4 wrong conjuncts? Under \"instead of\", \"that\" and \"the one\" are alternatives, not conjuncts. I'd call that L3: the coordination states the wrong relation between the two nominals.\n\nHmm, but is it \"wrong\" or just an encoding variance? The rules repeatedly forgive structural choices where grammar allows several. Grammar does not allow \"instead of\" as a coordinating conjunction. So the coordination is not a grammar-allowed reading. But the wire's conjunction field holds \"instead of\", so semantics are preserved...\n\nCompare with settled case: \"A coordination with what the verb acts on states a second thing acted on: L3\" — that's about the time phrase case. The spirit: a coordination asserts both things are acted on. Here a coordination of \"that\" and \"the one\" as complement of \"keep\" states both are kept, which contradicts \"instead of\". Though the conjunction word carries the correction...\n\nI think a fair reading: the conjunction word \"instead of\" is not a conjunction; the coordination thus mis-states the tie. I'll grade L3. Correction: use relation marker \"instead of\" from c2 to n4.\n\nHmm, but wait, careful: the note must quote words and name the wrong statement. \"'instead of the one we just discussed' is written as a coordination of 'that' and 'the one', which states both are kept; the words say one replaces the other.\"\n\nHmm, but is this overly strict? Risk either way. Let me consider the grading distribution intent: the sop and alt differ; the \"advanced\" set = spaCy and hmm-rules disagree. Both formalizers are LLM outputs probably. The judge grades sop only. Being too lenient = all L8; too strict = many rejections. The rules say \"Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information.\"\n\nA coordination labelled with conjunction \"instead of\" — I'd say the next layer reads \"keep that, instead of the one\" and continues. It's not misled. Hmm. But the coordination wire's meaning is \"wires put side by side\" with conjunction; with conjunction \"instead of\" the meaning is clear.\n\nI'll go L8 for n=54, to stay fair. Hmm, but then what about the nominal \"the one we just discussed\" tied to c3 — fine.\n\nHmm, hold on. Also check: k2 is used as c2's complement (complement=$k2). Does the schema allow a coordination as complement? \"complement holds the nominals the verb takes\" — a coordination of nominals as complement; plausible.\n\nDecision: L8.\n\nRow 21 (n=55): \"I'd prefer the male option, and give the avatar a hat now.\"\nSOP: c1 declarative aux \"'d\" verb prefer; k1 \"and\" c1 c2; c2 declarative verb give modifier \"now\" complement n3 n4. Hmm — \"give the avatar a hat now\" is a command; written as declarative without subject. Per settled case: \"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a `subject` and an `auxiliary`, in the form that auxiliary takes, may share them: a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite.\" Here c1 has subject \"I\" and auxiliary \"'d\" (= would). \"give\" is not in the form \"would\" takes (bare infinitive) — actually \"give\" IS the bare form after \"would\": \"I'd prefer ... and give ...\". Hmm, \"I'd prefer the male option, and give the avatar a hat now\" — with \"I'd\" the reading \"and [I'd] give\" is grammatical and is exactly the settled case: verb after \"and\" following a clause with subject and auxiliary, in the form that auxiliary takes → may share them, clause with no subject of its own, declarative. \"give\" is the form \"would\" takes. So declarative is allowed by that settled case. Also lowering as imperative passes too. L8.\n\nRow 22 (n=56): \"Make my avatar a man character, then put a scarf on the avatar.\" L8 (as seen). Check n2 \"a man character\" head character, dep a, man. Fine.\n\nContinue: 23-28.",
