    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Row 125 (n=311): \"Make my avatar a robot but blue.\"\nSOP: complement=$k1 = coordination \"but\" joining n2 \"a robot\" and n3 \"blue\" (head \"blue\"). Hmm: \"a robot but blue\" — coordination of \"a robot\" and \"blue\"? The conjuncts are nominals; n3 has head \"blue\". Is that acceptable? The settled case about \"flat and dry\" says a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause is a discourse word since the schema has no wire for that pair. Here \"a robot but blue\": blue is a modifier word describing the robot. Holding \"blue\" as a nominal in a coordination with \"a robot\" — is that \"two nominals merged\"/wrong wire? Hmm, \"a robot\" and \"blue\" are not two things; \"blue\" describes the robot.\n\nThe rules: L3 \"Words stand in the wrong wire... The words of one nominal are split between wires, or two nominals are merged.\" Here \"blue\" as a separate nominal coordinated with \"a robot\" — arguably a coordination of two wires the message does relate (but = contrast). Both are complements of \"make\": \"make my avatar a robot but blue\" — make X a robot, but blue... Hmm.\n\nActually, in this dataset there are messages \"Make my avatar a blue robot\" (n=326) and \"Make my avatar a robot but blue\" (n=311). The \"but blue\" pattern: a modifier-like adjective after \"but\". Reading \"blue\" as a nominal coordinated with \"a robot\" is a plausible encoding? It states \"my avatar a robot and blue\" i.e. two complements. I think a fair judge accepts: no information lost, the relation \"but\" is recorded, and \"blue\" as a nominal head is the schema's way (there is no wire for the pair of modifiers). Hmm, but the settled case says for \"flat and dry\" (two modifiers of one clause) the conjunction is a discourse word because the schema has no wire for that pair. Here the formalizer used a coordination wire — the schema does have a coordination wire that can hold nominals. \"a robot but blue\" — this is exactly the \"flat and dry\" pattern: two words predicating the same thing (robot + blue), one being a nominal complement and the other an adjective.\n\nIs holding \"blue\" as a nominal wrong? The rules say (settled): \"font bigger\", \"bar gap small\" etc. are modifiers of the clause in a part with no verb. And \"make the box red\": \"red\" is a modifier of the clause, since \"the box\" is a complement. That's a settled case! By analogy, in \"Make my avatar a robot but blue\", \"blue\" should be a modifier of the clause (modifier words). Here it's a nominal head in a coordination with \"a robot\", i.e., held in the wrong wire.\n\nDoes that lose information or state something wrong? It states that \"blue\" is a thing (nominal) coordinated with \"a robot\" — a coordination binds it to \"a robot\" as an equal conjunct, whereas \"make the box red\" says red is a modifier. Hmm. But the coordination with \"but\" arguably conveys \"the avatar is a robot but blue [instead]\" - the contrast reading. Actually \"a robot but blue\" means \"a robot, except blue\" — the color. As a coordination of two nominals, next layer reads \"a robot and blue\"? The \"but\" is recorded on the coordination.\n\nI'm torn. Note the settled case for \"make the box red\" explicitly: \"'red' is a `modifier` of the clause, since 'the box' is a complement.\" Here \"a robot\" is a complement of \"make\", so \"blue\" should be a modifier of the clause. Held as a nominal → wrong wire → L3. But is a coordination with \"but\" a grammar-allowed reading in \"several readings pass\"? Not listed.\n\nHmm, but also: the schema has no wire for coordinating two modifiers, but here one is a nominal and one an adjective.\n\nLet me consider the parallel row 127 (n=315) \"Use the robot but painted blue for my avatar.\" SOP: c1 imperative Use complement n1 \"the robot\"; k1 coordination \"but\" conjunct c1 conjunct c2, where c2 = declarative verb \"painted\" modifier \"blue\"; r1 \"for\" from c2 to n2 \"my avatar\". So \"painted\" is a verb of a declarative clause coordinated with the imperative — \"The robot but painted blue\" where \"painted\" is a participle.\n\nReading: \"use the robot but painted blue\" = \"use the robot but [use it] painted blue\"? The coordination of c1 (imperative) with c2 (declarative \"painted blue\") — a clause with no subject, coordinated with a command. Per settled case: \"move the star so it sits under the table\" — a participle clause \"painted blue\" describing the robot. Making it a declarative clause coordinated with the imperative... The case \"A participle ... is the `verb` of a `nonfinite` clause tied to the thing\". Writing it as `declarative` — that's a form error: a clause with the participle \"painted\" as verb and no subject should be `nonfinite`, not `declarative`. L1 lists \"Its `form` is wrong: a clear question written as `imperative` or `declarative`, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as `fragment`.\" Declarative vs nonfinite isn't in the L1 list explicitly, but \"declarative\" for a participle clause with no subject is a wrong form statement... Also the settled case \"Have the legend switched on\": the participle is the verb of a `nonfinite` clause tied to the clause or nominal. So declaring it `declarative` is wrong-ish. Hmm, L1 says form is wrong when it's imperative/declarative confusion or verbless-written-as-fragment. Declarative for \"painted blue\" also asserts a subject the words don't show (L4: a `subject` the words do not show — no subject is written though).\n\nAlso, \"blue\" as modifier of c2 — fine.\n\nAnd the coordination of the imperative with the declarative c2: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\" That was specifically for a clause that ends a relation with a marker. Not our case.\n\nHmm, this is getting deep. Let me evaluate 127 holistically: \"Use the robot but painted blue for my avatar.\" The circuit says: two coordinated clauses — \"use the robot\" (imperative) and \"painted blue for my avatar\" (declarative, verb \"painted\", modifier \"blue\", relation \"for\" to \"my avatar\"). The next layer reads that the robot is painted blue and it's for my avatar. Nothing is lost; \"the robot\" is the object of use; \"painted blue\" modifies it via the coordination. I'd pass it... but the form `declarative` for a participle clause is a bit off. Is the form \"wrong\" in the L1 sense? The L1 list enumerates cases; a participle clause with no subject written as declarative isn't among them; it's more like the reverse of \"a clause with a verb written as fragment\". I'll accept. Hmm.\n\nActually wait. Let me reconsider: some of these \"declarative\" readings are covered by the settled case: \"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a subject and an auxiliary... may share them: a clause with no subject of its own, declarative or nonfinite.\" Here c1 is imperative with no auxiliary — not that case. But \"Grammar allows both readings\" there.\n\nI need to make decisions and move on. Let me adopt a consistent, defensible policy:\n\nPass (L8) when: all words present in plausible wires, ties present, no clearly wrong verb/form, no content in discourse, no lost nominal or ungrammatical relation start.\n\nReject when a clear rule applies:\n- L1: noun as verb, clear command/question form inverted, auxiliary/negation misplaced.\n- L2: content word in discourse.\n- L3: nominal split/merged, wrong head/subject, wrong relation end/marker/start, linking word as modifier/dependent, wrong conjunct.\n- L4: invented subject/coordination/ellipsis.\n- L5: clause verb missing entirely.\n- L6: untied wire.\n\nUnder this policy:\n- n=311 \"Make my avatar a robot but blue\": \"blue\" as a nominal head in a coordination with \"a robot\". Is that \"two nominals merged\"? No, they're split. Is \"blue\" in a wrong wire? Compared to settled \"make the box red\" → red is a modifier of the clause. Here the coordination records the \"but\" link. Since the coordination is a legal wire and \"but\" is a real conjunction here, and \"blue\" as nominal is questionable... I'd lean L3: \"'blue' in 'a robot but blue' is held as a nominal conjoined with 'a robot', but it describes the robot\" — the settled case \"make the box red\" supports modifier of the clause. Hmm, but that case is about \"the box red\" without \"but\". With \"but\" the coordination wire needs two conjuncts, and the schema has no wire for coordinating a nominal with a modifier. So the formalizer's only options: modifier of clause (dropping \"but\" into discourse) or coordination. The settled case for \"flat and dry\" says conjunction between two modifiers → discourse word. Here the pair is nominal+modifier.\n\nI'll pass n=311. Rationale: plausible encoding, nothing lost, the \"but\" recorded. Keep momentum.\n\nRow 128 (n=319): \"Pick a robot but blue for me.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Pick discourse=\"but\" modifier=\"blue\" complement n1 (\"a robot\"); r1 \"for\" from c1 to n2 (\"me\"). \n\n\"but\" in discourse! Is \"but\" here a word said to the listener? No — it's a conjunction with content. L2: \"A word with content is held in `discourse`, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" The settled case says a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause is a discourse word — but here \"but\" joins \"a robot\" and \"blue\"; \"blue\" is held as a modifier of the clause, so \"but\" joins a nominal and a modifier, not two modifiers. Hmm, the settled case: \"So is a conjunction between two `modifier` words of one clause ('flat and dry'): the schema has no wire for that pair.\" If \"blue\" is a modifier of the clause and \"a robot\" is a complement nominal, this is not two modifier words. So \"but\" is not covered; it's a conjunction whose place is lost. That's L2.\n\nHmm, but one could argue \"but\" is close to the \"flat and dry\" case. The difference: \"a robot\" is a nominal. In \"flat and dry\" both are modifiers. I'd call L2: \"but\" is held as discourse although it joins the thing picked with the word that describes it. Hmm, is that a \"word with content\"? A conjunction has content (contrast). Yes, L2 seems right.\n\nHmm, but careful: L2 says \"A word with content is held in `discourse`, so its place in the sentence is lost.\" A conjunction's place is lost → the contrast between robot and blue is dropped. But it's mild: \"Pick a robot but blue for me\" — with \"blue\" as modifier of the clause, the meaning \"a robot, blue\" is still there; only the \"but\" contrast is lost. Is that a lost explicit information? The word \"but\" is kept in the circuit (in discourse), so it's not lost from the circuit, its syntactic place is lost. L2 is exactly about that.\n\nHmm, alternatively pass since the analogy to \"flat and dry\" is close (a conjunction between words describing one thing). Ugh.\n\nDecision: For n=319 I'll grade L2, since \"a robot\" is a nominal and the settled exemption covers only two modifier words; the formalizer itself put \"blue\" as a modifier of the clause, which makes \"but\" a conjunction without a wire. Hmm, but wait — the same formalizer for n=311 used a coordination. Inconsistent. For n=319, the reading \"Pick a robot, but blue\" — the contrast word held in discourse. I'll go L2 with correction: coordination with \"but\" (like n=311) or \"but\" as modifier? Let me write correction: \n\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative verb=\"Pick\" complement=$k1\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"a\" head=\"robot\"\n@n2 syntax.nominal head=\"blue\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"but\" conjunct=$n1 conjunct=$n2\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"for\" from=$c1 to=$n3\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"me\"\n\nThat mirrors n=311. OK.\n\nHmm, hmm. But am I over-rejecting? Let me think about which choice a careful human judge would make... The rule for \"flat and dry\" exists precisely because a judge rejected the discourse hold for a conjunction between two modifiers. It says \"So is a conjunction between two `modifier` words of one clause\". The word \"blue\" here IS held as a modifier word of c1, and \"but\" joins \"a robot\" (a nominal/complement)... Not two modifier words. So the exemption doesn't cover it; the general L2 rule applies. I'm fairly comfortable with L2.\n\nRow 126 (n=314): L8. Row 129 (n=322): L8. Row 130 (n=324): L8.\n\n131-136 next.",
