    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Row 131 (n=326): L8.\nRow 132 (n=327): \"Would it be possible to make my avatar a blue robot character?\" L8. (c2 nonfinite \"make\" tied by \"to\" from c1.) Fine.\nRow 133 (n=328): \"Can you switch my avatar to the robot but painted blue?\"\nSOP: c1 interrogative Can switch subject you complement my avatar; r1 to n3 \"the robot\"; k1 coordination \"but\" conjunct c1, c2 (nonfinite painted, modifier blue).\nCoordination joining an interrogative clause with a nonfinite clause. Settled case: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an `imperative` or `interrogative` clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\" BUT that case specifies \"such a clause\" = \"A clause that ends a relation with a marker\". Here c2 (\"painted blue\") does not end a relation with a marker; it's a participle clause with no marker. Hmm.\n\nSo is this coordination of interrogative c1 with nonfinite c2 wrong? The exclusion in the settled cases is specifically about a clause inside a relation. Here the relation r1 (\"to\") starts at c1 and ends at n3 (a nominal), not at c2. So the coordination joins c1 and c2. Is that acceptable? It says \"switch... but painted blue\" — coordination of the question with the participle clause. Hmm, a coordination of a question with a nonfinite clause: the schema's coordination is \"wires put side by side\". I think that's a plausible encoding of \"the robot but painted blue\" where \"but painted blue\" is predicated.\n\nBut strictly, the participle c2 should be tied to \"the robot\" (the thing described) — here it's coordinated with c1 (the whole question clause), not tied to n3. Does the message's link \"painted blue\" → \"robot\" survive? The next layer sees a coordination between the question and \"painted blue\" — the tie to \"the robot\" is loose. Is that L6 (\"Any relation to its clause or to the nominal it describes is enough\" — the settled case about participles: \"A participle or a subordinate clause tied to nothing is L6. Any relation to its clause or to the nominal it describes is enough.\")? c2 IS tied: by k1 to c1, its clause. So L6 doesn't apply. \n\nI'll pass L8.\n\nRow 134 (n=331): \"I think I'd like a robot but blue as my avatar.\"\nSOP: c1 think subject I; c2 declarative 'd like subject I complement k1 (coordination \"but\" n3 \"a robot\" n4 \"blue\"); r1 \"as\" from n4 to n5 \"my avatar\"; r2 from c1 to c2.\nHmm: r1 \"as my avatar\" starts at n4 (\"blue\") — that's odd: \"a robot but blue as my avatar\" — \"as my avatar\" should attach to the whole \"a robot ... \" i.e. the coordination or c2. Starting at \"blue\" only. Is that \"a start the words rule out\"? \"blue as my avatar\"? The relation start freedom: \"the clause, or any nominal of that clause that grammar allows\". Is \"blue\" a nominal that grammar allows for \"as my avatar\"? \"blue as my avatar\" reading: \"blue as my avatar\" — hmm, the avatar is blue? Weird.\n\nUgh. But it's a coordination conjunct; both n3 and n4 are complements of \"like\". The relation could start at c2 (allowed). Starting at n4 \"blue\" is grammatically dubious but not absurd (\"a robot but blue as my avatar\" — the whole coordinated phrase \"a robot but blue\" is what he'd like as avatar).\n\nDoes this mislead? It states \"blue as my avatar\" instead of \"a robot-but-blue as my avatar\" — a slight mis-tie. Under L3 \"a start the words rule out, such as a nominal of another clause\" — \"blue\" is a nominal of the same clause, grammar-plausible? I'd pass.\n\nHmm. Alternatively pass. Yes L8, keep moving.\n\nRow 135 (n=335): \"Give me the robot but painted blue for the avatar.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Give complement n1 me, n2 the robot; k1 \"but\" c1 c2 (nonfinite painted blue); r1 \"for\" from c2 to n3 the avatar. Pass L8 (similar to 133).\n\nRow 136 (n=336): \"Remember that I want a robot that is blue.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Remember; r1 \"that\" to c2; c2 declarative want subject I complement n2 \"a robot\"; r2 \"that\" from n2 to c3 (declarative \"is\" modifier blue). Relative clause tied to the nominal. Good. L8.\n\n137-142.",
