    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Row 137 (n=339): \"My avatar, a robot but blue, that is the figure I want.\"\nSOP: n2 \"a robot\" + n3 \"blue\" coordinated, r1 from n1 (avatar) to k1 (the coordination). Fine — the appositive ties to the avatar. L8.\n\nRow 138 (n=340): \"The avatar I use should be the robot in blue.\" r1 \"in\" from n3 to n4 \"blue\". Fine. L8.\n\nRow 139 (n=342): \"Use the robot but painted blue as my avatar in every tab.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Use complement n1; k1 \"but\" c1 c2 where c2 = declarative verb \"painted\" modifier blue; r1 \"as\" from c2 to n2; r2 \"in\" from c2 to n3.\nSame as n=315 but \"declarative\" instead of nonfinite. Hmm. n=315 (row 127) had declarative too. Consistency: I passed 127. Hmm, but wait: \"painted blue\" as a declarative clause with no subject: \"the robot but painted blue\" — as declarative, it asserts a clause without subject. The rule for participles: \"A participle that names or describes ... is the `verb` of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing.\" So the form should be nonfinite. Writing declarative = the form of the clause is wrong? L1's list: \"Its `form` is wrong: a clear question written as `imperative` or `declarative`, or the reverse; a clause with a verb written as `fragment`.\" A participle clause written as declarative isn't in that enumeration. But there's a strong case that a nonfinite clause written as declarative is a wrong form... but L1 enumerates specific wrong forms.\n\nHmm. Consider the analogous settled case: \"a verb after 'and' or 'then' that follows a clause with a `subject` and an `auxiliary`... may share them: a clause with no subject of its own, `declarative` or `nonfinite`. Written as a command, `imperative`, it passes too.\" That's for verbs after and/then. Here after \"but\" following a clause... c1 \"Use the robot\" is imperative with no subject and no auxiliary. So the sharing case doesn't apply.\n\nI think writing a participle clause coordinated with a command as `declarative` asserts something the words don't say (a finite clause). But mild. Both 127 and 139 are the same pattern. To be consistent I'd grade both the same. Let me decide: reject or pass?\n\nJudging question: \"Can the next layer continue from this circuit by completing it, or must it first undo a wrong statement?\" A declarative clause \"painted blue\" with the relation \"as my avatar\"/\"for my avatar\" — the next layer reads \"the robot, painted blue, as my avatar\". It can continue; the form label matters little. Nothing explicit is lost.\n\nI'll pass both 127 and 139 (L8).\n\nRow 140 (n=344): L8.\nRow 141 (n=345): \"Set my avatar to a robot but blue and keep that choice for every new session, so I do not have to ask again.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Set complement n1; r1 \"to\" n2 \"a robot\"; k1 coordination \"but\" + \"and\" conjunct c1, c2, c3; c2 fragment modifier \"blue\" ellipsis=c1. Hmm — c2 is a fragment sharing the verb of c1 (\"Set\") with \"blue\" as a modifier. So the coordination reads: \"set my avatar to a robot\" but (elided set) \"blue\"; then \"keep that choice...\". \n\nIs c2's ellipsis acceptable? \"A fragment that clearly shares a verb ('make A blue and B green') has no ellipsis or names the wrong clause\" — this is the reverse: c2 claims to share c1's verb. \"a robot but blue\" — does the fragment \"blue\" share \"set\"? Reading \"set my avatar to a robot, but [set it] blue\"? Hmm, \"blue\" is not a complement of \"set\" in the message; it's a modifier of the robot/avatar.\n\nIs this a wrong statement (L4: \"an `ellipsis` on a fragment that shares no verb\")? Perhaps. The words \"a robot but blue\" don't give \"set ... blue\" as a second action; \"blue\" describes. The fragment with ellipsis=$c1 says there's a second command \"set [it] blue\". Hmm — that's a statement not in the message? Ugh, it's marginal. Alternatively \"blue\" as modifier of the clause (settled \"make the box red\" style) would be right.\n\nBut also note r3 \"so\" from c3 to c4 — the \"so\" clause as a relation with marker \"so\" starting at c3. Fine (allowed).\n\nI'd lean L8 — the fragment holds \"blue\" adjacent to the clause it describes, ties are fine, nothing lost. But is it a \"wrong statement\"? It asserts an elided verb for \"blue\". Compare: L4 example \"ellipsis on a fragment that shares no verb ('move the box, and thanks')\". \"thanks\" shares no verb; \"blue\" in \"a robot but blue\"... does it share \"set\"? Grammatically \"but blue\" in \"set my avatar to a robot but blue\" is a bare adjective phrase modifying the robot, not a VP. Hmm.\n\nI'll pass — the reading is recoverable and complete; low confidence but keep it simple.\n\nRow 142 (n=346): same as n=98 pattern → L8.\n\n143-149 remaining.",
