    "text": "Row 143 (n=347): L8.\nRow 144 (n=348): L8.\nRow 145 (n=349): \"Please make the robot but painted blue my avatar, because...\"\nSOP: c1 imperative make complement n1 \"the robot\"; k1 coordination \"but\" conjunct c1, c2 (nonfinite painted blue complement n2 \"my avatar\"); r1 \"because\" from c1 to k2.\n\nHmm: \"make the robot but painted blue my avatar\" — c2 is a nonfinite clause \"painted blue my avatar\" where \"my avatar\" is complement. So the reading: \"make the robot, but painted blue my avatar\"? That splits: \"make ... my avatar\" — the complement \"my avatar\" is attached to c2 (painted) rather than to \"make\". The message: \"make [the robot but painted blue] [my avatar]\". In the circuit, \"make\" has only \"the robot\" as complement and \"my avatar\" is the complement of \"painted\". Is that a wrong statement? \"painted blue my avatar\" isn't grammatical. The nominal \"my avatar\" is the complement of the wrong clause → L3 (\"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\").\n\nHmm, but is it? \"make the robot ... my avatar\" — \"my avatar\" is what the robot becomes, object complement of \"make\". Assigning it to the participle clause c2 is wrong. I'd call L3.\n\nAlternatively forgiving: the coordination k1 joins c1 and c2 and \"my avatar\" is c2's complement, so the whole reads \"make the robot but [it is] painted blue my avatar\" — muddled.\n\nHmm, let me check what alt says for this row to see if there's a cleaner reading (and to use as correction).\n\nRow 146 (n=350): \"If you can, set my avatar to a robot that is blue and keep it for the next time I open the app on this machine.\"\nSOP: r3 \"that\" from n3 \"a robot\" to c3 (declarative \"is\" modifier blue) — relative clause. k1 \"and\" c2 c4. r5 \"on\" from n7 \"the app\" to n8 \"this machine\" — starts at the nominal the app: allowed. L8.\n\nRow 147 (n=351): like n=103. L8.\nRow 148 (n=352): L8.\nRow 149 (n=353): \"Choose a robot but blue as my avatar and do not change it unless I ask, even after a restart.\"\nSOP: c1 imperative Choose discourse=\"but\" modifier=\"blue\" complement n1 \"a robot\"; r1 \"as\" from c1 to n2; ... Same as n=319 → L2 (but held as discourse).\n\nHmm wait. In 149 \"but\" is again in discourse with \"blue\" a modifier of the clause. Same reasoning → L2. And in n=311 (row 125) a coordination was used → pass. Consistency is fine.\n\nHmm, but hold on. Let me reconsider n=319/n=353 once more. Is \"but\" a \"word with content held in discourse, so its place in the sentence is lost\"? The settled case explicitly allows a conjunction between two modifier words of one clause to be a discourse word. In n=353, \"blue\" is a modifier word of the clause and \"a robot\" is a nominal complement. Hmm, but one could argue \"but blue\" is a \"modifier run\" describing... no, \"a robot\" is a nominal.\n\nAlternatively, one might say: the message is \"a robot but blue\"; the formalizer put \"blue\" as a clause modifier (per the \"make the box red\" case) and had no wire for \"but\", so it followed the \"flat and dry\" precedent as closely as possible. A judge could accept.\n\nUgh. Decision point. The difference between \"flat and dry\" (both modifiers) and \"a robot but blue\" (nominal + modifier) is real but small. The L2 rule's harm: \"so its place in the sentence is lost\". Is \"but\"'s place lost? Yes — the contrast between robot and blue is not recorded anywhere but discourse. But nothing about the avatar's identity is lost: \"a robot\" + \"blue\" both present.\n\nWeigh: rejecting on this is a marginal call. The instruction says \"Judge fairly: a plausible or incomplete reading that the next layer can continue from is acceptable; reject only a wrong statement or lost explicit information.\" Is the loss of \"but\"'s structural place \"lost explicit information\"? The word \"but\" is still in the circuit (in discourse), so information isn't lost from the circuit. Its syntactic place is lost → that's exactly L2's wording though.\n\nHmm. L2 exists for cases like \"add the paragraph Please review\" where \"Please\" is content. For a conjunction... The settled case explicitly treats a conjunction between modifiers as discourse (fine). Extending slightly to \"a robot but blue\" seems within the spirit.\n\nI'll reverse: pass n=319 and n=353 (L8). Consistency and fairness favor this: the conjunction's meaning (contrast) is not lost; the two words it joins are both present and tied.\n\nHmm, but then \"blue\" as a modifier of the clause and \"a robot\" as complement: the next layer reads \"pick a robot, blue\" — fine, plausible.\n\nHmm, wait. But there's a subtlety for n=353: \"Choose a robot but blue as my avatar\" — r1 \"as\" from c1 to n2. Fine.\n\nOK: pass both. That leaves rejections at: n=33 (L2), n=101 (L1), n=165 (L2), and possibly n=349 (L3).\n\nOnly 3-4 rejections out of 150 seems very lenient. Let me double check some of the earlier rows I may have glossed over, especially the ones where sop differed from alt in suspicious ways. Since the pairs are near-identical messages across 5 semantic families, and the formalizer (same one) produced near-identical circuits, most are consistent. The anomalies I found: n=33 & n=165 (\"not\" in discourse), n=101 (\"figure\" as verb), n=27 vs n=281 same pattern, n=40 vs n=41 (empty fragment), n=54 (instead of coordination).\n\nLet me re-examine n=41 (row 14) and its clones (n=107, 174, 236, 295) more carefully, since an empty fragment is unusual: c5 fragment ellipsis=c2 with no words, joined as a third conjunct, and r2 \"instead of\" from c5 to c4.\n\nUnder \"What must be right\" #3: \"Whether a fragment shares the verb of another clause (`ellipsis`).\" c5 has no words, shares \"keep\". Is that wrong? A clause with no words is not a clause of the message. L4: \"The circuit states what the message does not say: `ellipsis` on a fragment that shares no verb\". Setting aside that example, is there a rule \"a clause must hold words\"? The program \"has already checked that every word of the message stands in exactly one value\" — an empty wire doesn't violate that.\n\nBut consider: \"instead of asking me again\" — the relation could start at c2. Instead the formalizer invented c5 to represent the elided \"keep\" and made the relation start at c5. And k1 joins c1, c2, c5 — a coordination with an empty conjunct. That is a structure the message doesn't have: there aren't three coordinated clauses; \"instead of asking me again\" is an adjunct.\n\nIs this \"a coordination between wires that the message does not relate\" (L4)? Marginal. Honestly I'd rather judge one of these as L3/L6? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, a stricter view: \"instead of asking me again\" modifies \"keep\", and the formalizer's r2 starts at c5 (=an elided copy of c2). Rather than starting at c2 directly, it inserted a phantom clause. The next layer can still see the tie to \"keep\" (via ellipsis=c2). I'll keep L8 but I want to double-check there's no better candidate for L6: is anything in the message tied to nothing? In n=41: all words present? \"Could you set my avatar to the man and keep that figure every time I start a new drawing, instead of asking me again?\" Words: Could you set my avatar to the man and keep that figure every time I start a new drawing instead of asking me again — yes all present, all tied.\n\nFine, L8.\n\nNow n=349. Let me check the alt for its reading before deciding.\n\nAlso, I should double check a couple of rows where \"sop\" has a suspicious structure I might have skimmed: row 8 handled; row 37 handled. Let me check rows where the c-list has something odd. I have seen all rows' sops now. Let me review my quick passes for suspicious items:\n\n1. n=32/n=97/etc (k1 with two conjunctions \"and\"+\"so\" joining three conjuncts) — plausible.\n2. n=38/104/171/233/352 (c5 \"start a new drawing\" declarative without subject, coordinated \"or\" with c4) — acceptable.\n3. n=36/102/169/231/350: r5 from n5 (\"the next time\") to c4 — fine.\n4. n=54 style: k2 coordination \"instead of\" — I decided L8.\n5. n=55/n=121 etc.: c2 declarative \"give\" — passes per settled case (auxiliary sharing)... wait, in n=55 \"I'd prefer the male option, and give the avatar a hat now.\" c1 declarative with auxiliary \"'d\" and subject \"I\"; c2 declarative verb give no subject. The settled case requires the verb to be \"in the form that auxiliary takes\" — \"give\" is the base form after \"would\" → yes. Passes.\n\n6. n=91/n=159/n=283/n=223/etc: \"The avatar I use should be the woman.\" — c1 declarative use subject n2 \"I\" — wait, c1 \"use\" has subject \"I\" and no complement; r1 from n1 \"The avatar\" to c1. Good.\n\n7. n=101 (rejected, \"figure\" as verb) — check the parallel rows: n=38? no. The \"Please make the X my avatar, because...\" pattern appears at n=101 (sop wrong), n=168 (correct), n=230 (correct), n=289 (correct), n=349 (\"but painted blue\" variant). Good - only 101 has the error.\n\n8. n=171 vs n=104 etc: fine.\n\n9. n=311 (k1 coordination of complement k1) — passed.\n\n10. n=345 (n=141) fragment \"blue\" ellipsis=c1 — passed.\n\nLet me double check n=139 (n=\"342\") and n=315 (127): \"declarative\" participle. Passed.\n\nNow, some other checks — grammatical details I might have missed:\n\n- n=24 \"Remember that I want the male option.\" → c1 imperative verb Remember with no complement; relation \"that\" from c1 to c2. Good.\n- n=27 \"My avatar, a man, that is the figure I want.\" — c3 fragment complement n1; k1 c3+c1; r1 n1→n2. Also is n2 (\"a man\") tied? via r1. Yes. L8.\n\n- n=45/112/179/239 \"the X it is.\" L8.\n\n- n=46 \"a man from now on.\" — fragment; r1 from n1 to n2 — relation starting at the nominal. Hmm, \"a man from now on\": the relation \"from now\" starts at n1. Then \"on\" is a modifier of the clause. Also c1 has complement n1 and r1 from n1. Hmm, is \"on\" a modifier of a fragment clause? Fine.\n\nBut hmm, one thing: is n1 both complement of c1 and the start of r1? Yes, allowed.\n\nNow let me reconsider whether \"From now on\" split readings should be rejected for the reason \"A linking word is held as a modifier\" (L3): \"on\" is a preposition. In \"from now on\", \"on\" is not linking anything (no object). I'll stay with L8.\n\nLet me now check the alt for n=349 to see if it offers a cleaner correction, then write p3.\n\nActually, for n=349, let me reconsider whether C2's taking \"my avatar\" as complement is really wrong. Text: \"Please make the robot but painted blue my avatar, because ...\"\nReading in the circuit: c1 \"make\" complement \"the robot\"; k1 coordinates c1 with c2; c2 nonfinite \"painted\" modifier \"blue\" complement \"my avatar\".\n\nSo \"my avatar\" hangs on \"painted\": \"painted blue my avatar\" — ungrammatical. The message's \"make X Y\" frame is broken: \"make the robot ... my avatar\" — the formalizer put \"my avatar\" in the wrong clause. L3 (\"A nominal is the complement of the wrong clause\"). I'm reasonably confident. Grade L3, and write my own correction:\n\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative discourse=\"Please\" verb=\"make\" complement=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"robot\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"but\" conjunct=$n1 conjunct=$c2 ... hmm, better: use a nonfinite clause tied to n1 via relation.\n\nSimplest valid: \n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative discourse=\"Please\" verb=\"make\" complement=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"robot\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"painted\" modifier=\"blue\"\n@k1 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"but\" conjunct=$n1 conjunct=$c2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"my\" head=\"avatar\"\n@r1 syntax.relation marker=\"because\" from=$c1 to=$k2\n@n3 syntax.nominal head=\"that\"\n@c3 syntax.clause form=declarative verb=\"is\" subject=$n3\n@c4 syntax.clause form=declarative modifier=\"how\" verb=\"know\" modifier=\"here\" subject=$n4 complement=$n5\n@n4 syntax.nominal head=\"people\"\n@n5 syntax.nominal head=\"me\"\n@k2 syntax.coordination conjunction=\"and\" conjunct=$c3 conjunct=$c5\n@n6 syntax.nominal head=\"I\"\n@c5 syntax.clause form=declarative auxiliary=\"do\" negation=\"not\" verb=\"want\" subject=$n6\n@n7 syntax.nominal head=\"it\"\n@r2 syntax.relation marker=\"to\" from=$c5 to=$c6\n@c6 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"change\" subject=$n7\n@r3 syntax.relation from=$c3 to=$c4\n\nWait: a coordination joining n1 (nominal) with c2 (clause) — is that ok? Hmm, better to instead attach c2 to n1 by a relation (like \"the robot, painted blue\"). The settled case: \"A participle that names or describes ('a group called g2', 'a bird facing left') is the `verb` of a nonfinite clause tied to the thing.\" So a relation from n1 to c2 works. Let me use that:\n\n@c1 syntax.clause form=imperative discourse=\"Please\" verb=\"make\" complement=$n1 complement=$n2\n@n1 syntax.nominal dependent=\"the\" head=\"robot\"\n@c2 syntax.clause form=nonfinite verb=\"painted\" modifier=\"blue\"\n@r4 syntax.relation from=$n1 to=$c2\n@n2 syntax.nominal dependent=\"my\" head=\"avatar\"\n... (rest)\n\nThat's clean: \"make the robot (painted blue) my avatar\". The \"but\" — hmm, then \"but\" has no place! \"but\" must stand in exactly one value. Put it as a modifier of c1? Or as marker in the relation r4: marker=\"but\"? \"the robot but painted blue\" → relation with marker \"but\" from n1 to c2. That's elegant: marker \"but\". Is \"but\" a linking word? Here it links the participle. Hmm, allowed? Alternatively make \"but\" a modifier of c2 (a clause modifier). I'll put \"but\" as the marker of the relation r4 — plausible.\n\nActually safer: modifier of c2. Hmm, \"A word that grades a linking word\" case aside... I'll use marker=\"but\" in the relation, mirroring that \"but\" introduces the participial modifier.\n\nOK now also double-check row 145's other oddity: n2 \"my avatar\" is dependent of... fine in my correction.\n\nLet me now write p3: rows 83..149 (n=222 ... n=353) with the rejection for n=349.\n\nWait — also need to double check rows 83-149 for rejections I decided: none except n=349. And n=319, n=353 → L8 (reversed decision).\n\nLet me write p3 now.\n\nRow list for p3 (indices 83..149):\n83 222\n84 223\n85 228\n86 229\n87 230\n88 231\n89 232\n90 233\n91 234\n92 235\n93 236\n94 239\n95 240\n96 246\n97 247\n98 250\n99 251\n100 264\n101 265\n102 268\n103 269\n104 271\n105 278\n106 281\n107 282\n108 283\n109 287\n110 288\n111 289\n112 290\n113 292\n114 294\n115 295\n116 296\n117 299\n118 300\n119 302\n120 304\n121 305\n122 306\n123 307\n124 309\n125 311\n126 314\n127 315\n128 319\n129 322\n130 324\n131 326\n132 327\n133 328\n134 331\n135 335\n136 336\n137 339\n138 340\n139 342\n140 344\n141 345\n142 346\n143 347\n144 348\n145 349 (L3)\n146 350\n147 351\n148 352\n149 353\n\nThat's 67 rows.\n\nBefore writing, let me reconsider a couple I rushed: \n\nRow 132 (n=327): \"Would it be possible to make my avatar a blue robot character?\" — sop: c1 interrogative aux Would verb be modifier possible subject n1 \"it\"; r1 \"to\" c1→c2; c2 nonfinite make complement n2 n3. Good. L8.\n\nRow 143 (n=347): fine.\n\nRow 133 (n=328): \"Can you switch my avatar to the robot but painted blue?\" — k1 joins c1 (interrogative) with c2 (nonfinite). The settled case: \"A command or a question cannot stand inside another clause, so a coordination that joins an imperative or interrogative clause with such a clause has a wrong conjunct: L3.\" — for a clause that ends a relation with a marker. Here c2 doesn't end a relation with a marker. Hmm, but the spirit: coordinating a question with a participle clause. The coordination is \"switch... but painted blue\" — I think fine; the participle is tied to its clause via k1.\n\nHmm, wait. There is a subtlety: does k1 join at the right level? \"the robot but painted blue\" — \"but painted blue\" describes \"the robot\". Coordinating c2 with c1 (the whole question) rather than tying c2 to n3 (the robot) is a slight mis-tie, but the participle is tied to something (L6 avoided).\n\nFine, L8.\n\nRow 135 (n=335): similar. L8.\n\nRow 136 (n=336): fine.\n\nRow 138 (n=340): fine. Row 140 (n=344): fine.\n\nRow 146 (n=350): r5 \"on\" from n7 (\"the app\") to n8 (\"this machine\") — start at a nominal (the app) — allowed. And c5 open subject n6 \"I\" complement n7; r6 from n5 (\"the next time\") to c5. Fine.\n\nRow 148 (n=352): fine.\n\nOK, write p3.",
