    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "Just ordering: p1 had n=33 appended at the end. Let me sort the final file by n (CSV order = ascending n).\n\nBefore finalizing, let me do a focused second pass on a few remaining borderline patterns to avoid being uniformly lenient. Given 150 rows and only 4 rejections, let me re-examine those patterns I rationalized:\n\n1. n=54 group (120, 187, 247, 306): coordination `conjunction=\"instead of\"` joining \"that\" and \"the one\". L3 says \"A coordination has a wrong conjunct.\" Hmm. The coordination wire here does have conjunction \"instead of\" which is not a coordinating conjunction. The settled case about \"The earlier wire a coordination joins...\" doesn't help. What would the judge do? The message: \"keep that instead of the one we just discussed\" — the coordination of \"that\" and \"the one\" as the object of \"keep\" says both are kept. The \"instead of\" word flags exclusion. Since the conjunction field is part of the coordination and carries the word, the meaning is recoverable. I'll keep L8.\n\n2. Pattern: relation start at a nominal in cases where \"of\"/\"for\" attachments... e.g. n=31 \"as my avatar\" from n2. Fine.\n\n3. n=141/345 fragment \"blue\" with ellipsis=c1. Keep L8.\n\n4. n=40 (n=41) empty fragment c5. Keep L8.\n\n5. n=127/139/315/342: participle as `declarative`. Keep L8.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider #5 once more, since it occurs 2-4 times and the L1 rule about form... The `form` list is imperative/declarative/interrogative/fragment/nonfinite; a participle clause written declarative is a wrong form-ish. But L1's enumeration of wrong form cases doesn't include it, and L1 is \"A clause that the circuit writes is wrong. Its form is wrong: [enumeration]\". A strict reading: the enumeration defines when form is wrong. So not L1.\n\nOK. But wait — I should double check a real risk: did I misread any row where the sop has an obviously wrong subject? Let me scan a few quickly for \"subject=\" mismatches... I read all sops; issues would have been visible.\n\nOne more: row 21 (n=55) — \"I'd prefer the male option, and give the avatar a hat now.\" c2 declarative \"give\" no subject. Settled case permits (auxiliary 'd). OK.\n\nRow 141 (n=345) k1 conjunction \"but\"+\"and\" conjuncts c1, c2(fragment blue, ellipsis=c1), c3. Also r3 \"so\" from c3 to c4 — so the \"so I do not have to ask again\" is a relation. Fine.\n\nLet me also reconsider rows 8/59 more carefully — could the \"not\" in discourse be judged L2? Yes I did.\n\nNow, is there any row where a whole clause's verb is missing (L5)? I didn't spot one.\n\nIs there a row where a nominal is tied to nothing (L6)? Let me verify programmatically: check each sop that every wire appears as an endpoint of some relation/coordination or is a top-level clause... That's complex. Instead, let me programmatically list, for each sop, wires that are never referenced by any relation/coordination AND are not clause-level; and clauses referenced by nothing. Then eyeball whether any of those indicates a real L6 (e.g., a nominal meant to attach to something).\n\nLet me write a quick checker: for each row's sop, parse @id and references ($...). Report ids referenced nowhere, with their content, and the text. That could reveal missed L6s. But careful: a main clause is normally referenced by nothing (fine). A nominal that's a complement of a clause is \"referenced\" by the clause's key (not a $ref). Hmm, my parser should collect all references including those inside clause fields (complement=$n1).\n\nSo: unreferenced ids = those not appearing as a value anywhere. For a nominal that is a complement, it appears in the clause's complement= list → referenced. So a truly orphan nominal would show up. Let me check.\n\nAlso check the reverse: does every word appear? The upstream program checked that, supposedly for the original sop; my corrections I should verify manually. Let me verify my 4 corrections' word coverage.\n\nLet me run the orphan check.",
