    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "The file is written correctly: 150 lines, order matches CSV, 146 L8, 4 rejections with corrections.\n\nNow, I'm uneasy about the 146/4 split. Let me do one honest second look at a sample of rows I passed quickly, specifically hunting for L3-type errors in relation starts and nominal heads, since those are the most common real defects. Let me pick a handful of rows and check heads carefully against the text:\n\n1. n=311 \"Make my avatar a robot but blue.\" — n1 \"my avatar\" head avatar ✓; n2 \"a robot\" ✓; n3 head blue.\n2. n=331 \"I think I'd like a robot but blue as my avatar.\" — r1 \"as\" from n4 (blue) to n5 (my avatar). Hmm, I passed. Is that a \"start the words rule out\"? \"blue as my avatar\" is not a grammatical attachment; \"a robot but blue as my avatar\" — the \"as my avatar\" attaches to the coordinated thing. Starting at n4 makes \"blue\" the thing that is my avatar... it's the same coordinated pair though. Eh.\n\n3. The repeated pattern in the -advanced set that made it \"advanced\" (spaCy vs hmm-rules disagree) suggests these messages are genuinely ambiguous, hence all the L8.\n\nGiven the rules' explicit fairness instruction and the settled cases that bless most of these constructions, I'll stand by my grades. The four rejections are all clear-cut or well-supported by explicit rule text.\n\nLet me spot check the final file's rejection lines render correctly (with escaped \\n inside sop) and confirm no stray file was created besides the verdicts. Also check I didn't create files in the working folder (only the verdicts).",
