    "relatedEntryId": null,
    "type": "reasoning",
    "text": "The file is in place. Only that new file is created in the drawing folder (other untracked files pre-existed from other sessions, e.g. objects-01-simple.p01.verdicts.jsonl — not mine; I didn't touch it).\n\nNow, I'm concerned that 146 L8 is too lenient. Let me do a genuine second pass focused on a sample of rows with fresh eyes, looking for defects I might have rationalized. Given the messages repeat in 5 families with near-identical sops, defects are family-level, not row-level. So I should verify the family templates once each — which I did — plus the odd variants (which is where the 4 rejections came from).\n\nFamily templates (all verified as plausible):\nA. \"From now on, my avatar is X.\" + variants (keep it as X from here on / use X for the avatar from now on / X it is / a man from now on / I changed my mind: X / the figure I want is X / I am happiest with X as my avatar / Remember that I want X / The avatar I use should be X / The avatar should be X / Have/Lets ... / My avatar, X, that is the figure I want).\nB. \"Set my avatar to X and keep that choice..., so I do not have to ask again.\"\nC. \"I want my avatar to be X for every session from now on, not only for the diagram...\"\nD. \"Whatever else changes on the page, ... until I tell you otherwise.\"\nE. \"If you can, set ... and keep it for the next time I open the app on this machine.\"\nF. \"I would like my avatar to be X, so please set it and keep it for later instead of resetting it.\"\nG. \"Make my avatar the X; that is the figure I want every time I open the app or start a new drawing.\"\nH. \"What ever I do in the diagram tab, ... because I do not want to change it every morning.\"\nI. \"Please make the X my avatar, because that is how people know me here ...\" (n=101 error, others fine)\nJ. \"Could you set my avatar to X and keep that figure every time I start a new drawing, instead of asking me again?\"\nK. \"Choose X as my avatar and do not change it unless I ask, even after a restart.\"\nL. \"I'd prefer X, and give the avatar a hat now.\"\nM. \"Actually, set the avatar to X and keep that instead of the one we just discussed.\"\nN. \"Make my avatar X, then put a scarf on the avatar.\"\nO. \"My avatar should be X, and also change the avatar's shirt to green.\"\nP. \"From now on use X, and then give the avatar a crown for today.\"\nQ. \"I think I'd like a robot but blue as my avatar.\" (only n=331)\nR. \"No, I meant the robot character.\" (n=302)\nS. \"Make my avatar the robot instead; ...\" (n=292)\nT. \"the robot instead it is.\" (n=299)\n\nAll consistent. So L8 heavy is plausible for this set — the sops appear to come from a strong formalizer. Fine.\n\nI'm done. Let me finish the todo list and report.\n\nActually one more check: the instruction says \"You may take alt as the corrected circuit when it is right; otherwise write your own.\" I wrote my own for all 4. Fine.\n\nAlso confirm my corrections parse/no duplicate ids and reference correctly. Quick validation: check that in each correction every $-ref exists. Let me run a validator on the 4 sops.",
